COMMAND NAME: mppinitstandby.sh

Adds and/or initializes a standby MPP master host for a Greenplum Database system.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mppinitstandby.sh -s <standby_hostname> [-i] [-n] [-a] [-q] 
[-l <logfile_directory>]

mppinitstandby.sh [-? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mppinitstandby.sh script will create a backup MPP master host 
if one has not already been created for your Greenplum Database system. Before 
running this script, make sure that the Greenplum Database software is installed 
on the backup master host and that you have exchanged SSH keys between 
hosts. See the "Greenplum Database Administrator Guide" for instructions.

The script will perform the following steps:

* Shutdown your Greenplum Database system

* Update the MPP system catalog to add the new backup master host 
  information (use the -n option to skip this step)

* Setup the backup master instance on the alternate master host

* Start the synchronization process (if the -i option is supplied)

* Restart your Greenplum Database system

A backup MPP master host serves as a `warm standby' in the event of 
the primary MPP master host becoming unoperational. The backup master 
is kept up to date by a transaction log replication process (mppsyncmaster), 
which runs on the backup master host and keeps the data between the 
primary and backup master hosts synchronized. If the primary master fails, 
the log replication process is shutdown, and the backup master can be 
activated in its place by using the mppactivatestandby.sh script. Upon 
activation of the backup master, the replicated logs are used to reconstruct 
the state of the MPP master host at the time of the last successfully 
committed transaction.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-s <standby_hostname>

Required. The host name of the standby MPP master host.

-i

This will start the synchronization process (mppsyncmaster) between 
the backup and primary MPP master hosts. The default is to not start 
the synchronization process.

-n

Use this option if you already have a standby MPP master configured, 
and just want to resynchronize the data between the primary and backup 
master host. The MPP system catalog tables will not be updated.

-a

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.

-q

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but 
is still written to the log file.

-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/mppAdminLogs.

-?

Displays the online help.

-v

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.

*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Add a backup MPP master host to your Greenplum Database system:

mppinitstandby.sh -s host09


Add a backup MPP master host to your Greenplum Database system and start the data 
synchronization process with the primary MPP master host:

mppinitstandby.sh -s host09 -i


Start an existing backup MPP master host and synchronize the data with 
the primary master host - do not add a new MPP backup master host to 
the system catalog:

mppinitstandby.sh -s host09 -i -n


